<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Megali Idea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Great Idea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Megali Idea, or Great Idea, was the aim of Greek nationalism to unite all Greek-inhabited lands in one state, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as its capital. Named in a speech by Ioannis Kolettis in 1844, it guided Greek policy for nearly eighty years and led to the gain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, Macedonia and other lands. It ended with the defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> in 1922 and the exchange of populations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/megali-idea.jpg" alt="Megali Idea" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Signed by Gr. Kouriotis (Γρ. Κουριώτης) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Great_Greece_(Megali_Hellas)_Venizelos_c1920.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
